Our focus is primarily on the original jurisdiction of the United States district courts. Specifically, we will focus on the two most common bases for original jurisdiction: federal question jurisdiction and diversity jurisdiction. We will then turn to additional bases for the exercise of subject matter jurisdiction known as supplemental jurisdiction and removal procedure.

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON FEDERAL QUESTION JURISDICTION
Article III, Section 2 extends the judicial power of the United States to cases “arising under this Constitution, the Laws of the United States, and Treaties.” This means that for federal courts created by Congress, Congress has the power to grant those courts subject matter jurisdiction over cases “arising under” the Constitution, laws, and treaties of the United States. This is known as federal question jurisdiction. The primary statute by which Congress granted federal question jurisdiction to the district courts is 28 U.S.C. § 1331.

28 U.S.C. § 1331
Federal Question

The district courts shall have original jurisdiction of all civil actions arising under the Constitution, laws, or treaties of the United States.



•Federal district courts have original jurisdiction over actions that arise under the Constitution, law, and treaties of the United States.

