NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON FEDERAL QUESTION JURISDICTION
1. In what respects does the text of § 1331 diverge from the text of Article III? In what respects is it identical?

2. Federal question jurisdiction refers to the power of the federal courts to hear cases that involve issues of federal law. But does any connection between a case and federal law create federal question jurisdiction? What does it mean for a case to “arise under” federal law? These will be questions we will explore in detail in cases such as Osborn v. Bank of the United States, Louisville & Nashville Railroad v. Mottley, and Gunn v. Minton.

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON DIVERSITY JURISDICTION
In addition to federal question jurisdiction, Article III, Section 2 extends the judicial power of the United States to cases “between Citizens of different States.” When the parties to a case are citizens of different states, this is known as diversity of citizenship, and federal subject matter jurisdiction over cases with diversity of citizenship is known as diversity jurisdiction. The primary statute codifying Congress’s grant of diversity jurisdiction to the federal district courts is 28 U.S.C. § 1332.

28 U.S.C. § 1332
Diversity of citizenship

(a) The district courts shall have original jurisdiction of all civil actions where the matter in controversy exceeds the sum or value of $75,000, exclusive of interest and costs, and is between—

(1) citizens of different States;

(2) citizens of a State and citizens or subjects of a foreign state, * * * ; [or]

(3) citizens of different States and in which citizens or subjects of a foreign state are additional parties; * * *

